1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:02,689
Hi, we're the Creed A.I team.

2
00:00:02,689 --> 00:00:12,850
Small business owners hear about A.I tools every day, but they don't have time to figure out which one actually fits a restaurant, a clinic or an HVAC shop.

3
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:15,629
Review sites tell you what people liked.

4
00:00:15,629 --> 00:00:24,250
They don't tell you whether a tool does your specific job, what it will really cost you each month, or whether you're already paying for something that covers it.

5
00:00:24,900 --> 00:00:26,164
So we built Creed A.I.

6
00:00:26,164 --> 00:00:34,377
You tell it your business and the job you need done, and it shows tools that were actually checked for that job, with the price and the source.

7
00:00:34,377 --> 00:00:38,340
And when you want to talk it through, there's Arkadio, our assistant.

8
00:00:38,990 --> 00:00:47,178
We're Arvind, who built Arkadio and its security guardrails, including testing against the OWASP Top 10 for A.I applications.

9
00:00:47,178 --> 00:00:51,108
Giri, who designed the interface and the sign-in experience.

10
00:00:51,108 --> 00:00:54,710
And Aurin, who built the core app and its architecture.

11
00:00:55,360 --> 00:00:57,810
Let's walk through it, as a restaurant owner.

12
00:00:58,460 --> 00:01:00,439
Here are some common situations.

13
00:01:00,439 --> 00:01:06,437
Each slide is a real kind of business with a real problem, like missed calls or staff scheduling.

14
00:01:06,437 --> 00:01:18,620
Nineteen kinds of business are covered today, from coffee shops and bakeries to plumbers, salons and clinics, along with twelve everyday jobs, like missed calls, invoicing, reviews and bookkeeping.

15
00:01:19,270 --> 00:01:20,906
Let's be a restaurant owner.

16
00:01:20,906 --> 00:01:22,308
I type my business here.

17
00:01:22,308 --> 00:01:24,411
Now I pick a task, staff scheduling.

18
00:01:24,411 --> 00:01:26,865
Notice the business still says Restaurant.

19
00:01:26,865 --> 00:01:28,734
That's one of the bugs we fixed.

20
00:01:28,734 --> 00:01:29,961
I'll come back to it.

21
00:01:29,961 --> 00:01:32,941
You don't need to know any software names to start.

22
00:01:32,941 --> 00:01:38,900
Every field is optional, and if your problem isn't on the list, you can describe it in your own words.

23
00:01:39,550 --> 00:01:43,645
The top tells me there's one recommended tool and three more options.

24
00:01:43,645 --> 00:01:50,470
It also tells me exactly which business and which task these results are for, so I always know what I'm looking at.

25
00:01:51,120 --> 00:01:53,074
Recommended means one thing only.

26
00:01:53,074 --> 00:01:57,100
We checked this tool for this exact task, for this kind of business.

27
00:01:57,100 --> 00:02:04,678
You see the monthly price, and if you open details, every fact links to the vendor page it came from and the date we checked it.

28
00:02:04,678 --> 00:02:09,770
For a restaurant, that's Homebase, which has a free plan that covers staff scheduling.

29
00:02:10,420 --> 00:02:12,868
Everything else goes under More options.

30
00:02:12,868 --> 00:02:14,092
Each card is simple:

31
00:02:14,092 --> 00:02:19,967
the name, a one-line summary, a link to the website, a button to compare and a heart to save it.

32
00:02:19,967 --> 00:02:24,251
We don't stamp these as good fits, because we haven't proven that yet.

33
00:02:24,251 --> 00:02:27,249
Here, that's 7shifts, Connecteam and When I Work:

34
00:02:27,249 --> 00:02:30,860
real alternatives you can compare, but not recommendations.

35
00:02:31,510 --> 00:02:33,664
I can put up to three side by side.

36
00:02:33,664 --> 00:02:40,311
I choose the tasks I care about and the table shows which tool covers which task and the added monthly cost.

37
00:02:40,311 --> 00:02:45,112
If I already pay for a tool, it tells me so instead of making me buy it twice.

38
00:02:45,112 --> 00:02:50,220
And every number in that table comes from the same sourced facts, not from the A.I.

39
00:02:50,870 --> 00:02:54,800
Here's a case where nothing has been checked yet for this task.

40
00:02:54,800 --> 00:03:00,851
Instead of a zero recommended message or a page full of question marks, you get Tools to look at:

41
00:03:00,851 --> 00:03:03,970
real tools in this area with a link to their site.

42
00:03:03,970 --> 00:03:05,530
Honest, and still useful.

43
00:03:06,180 --> 00:03:07,760
Now, meet Arkadio.

44
00:03:08,410 --> 00:03:09,292
This is Arkadio.

45
00:03:09,292 --> 00:03:13,097
You can talk to it the way you'd talk to a friend who knows software.

46
00:03:13,097 --> 00:03:16,846
Arkadio reads our catalog live through a tool call, not from memory.

47
00:03:16,846 --> 00:03:21,202
Its answer comes with the same cards you saw before, and the same rule applies.

48
00:03:21,202 --> 00:03:24,400
It only calls something recommended when we've checked it.

49
00:03:24,400 --> 00:03:32,450
You can start with a few quick questions about your business, or just type what's slowing you down, like, I keep missing calls while I'm on a job.

50
00:03:33,100 --> 00:03:35,241
Here's the part we care most about.

51
00:03:35,241 --> 00:03:38,239
Arkadio cannot just make up a price or a feature.

52
00:03:38,239 --> 00:03:49,129
Before its answer reaches you, our server checks that it really called the catalog tool, that the result matches what we expected, and that every claim points to stored evidence.

53
00:03:49,129 --> 00:03:52,372
If any of that fails, you don't get a made up answer.

54
00:03:52,372 --> 00:03:53,840
You get a safe fallback.

55
00:03:53,840 --> 00:03:56,226
It also has guardrails on what goes in.

56
00:03:56,226 --> 00:04:01,426
If someone tries to talk it out of its rules, it politely refuses, like you see here.

57
00:04:01,426 --> 00:04:09,013
And if someone pastes a card number, a Social Security number, or a password, it's stopped before it ever reaches the model.

58
00:04:09,013 --> 00:04:16,660
Arvind tested Arkadio against every item in the OWASP Top 10 for A.I applications, with fifty-four automated security checks.

59
00:04:17,310 --> 00:04:23,034
If you sign in, you can save plans and favourites, and your chat history comes back next time.

60
00:04:23,034 --> 00:04:25,044
That's tied to your account only.

61
00:04:25,044 --> 00:04:26,506
Nobody else can read it.

62
00:04:26,506 --> 00:04:33,570
Passwords need eight to fourteen characters and at least one number, and a forgotten password can be reset by email.

63
00:04:34,220 --> 00:04:35,710
So how does it all work?

64
00:04:36,360 --> 00:04:37,825
Here's how it's built.

65
00:04:37,825 --> 00:04:43,749
It runs on one Azure virtual machine in Belgium Central, as nine Docker Compose services.

66
00:04:43,749 --> 00:04:45,347
Caddy is the front door.

67
00:04:45,347 --> 00:04:47,877
It handles HTTPS and security headers.

68
00:04:47,877 --> 00:04:50,340
The FastAPI app holds the core logic:

69
00:04:50,340 --> 00:04:53,468
matching, comparison, plans and sign-in checks.

70
00:04:53,468 --> 00:04:55,466
The matching is deterministic.

71
00:04:55,466 --> 00:04:58,395
The model never decides what is recommended.

72
00:04:58,395 --> 00:05:01,457
Hermes is the agent runtime that runs Arkadio.

73
00:05:01,457 --> 00:05:04,852
The MCP executor is the only tool Arkadio can call.

74
00:05:04,852 --> 00:05:08,247
It's read-only and it gives public catalog details.

75
00:05:08,247 --> 00:05:10,111
Every call leaves a receipt.

76
00:05:10,111 --> 00:05:13,107
The model gateway meters every model request.

77
00:05:13,107 --> 00:05:16,968
It reserves the cost before the call and settles it after.

78
00:05:16,968 --> 00:05:19,564
That's how we keep a hard spending cap.

79
00:05:19,564 --> 00:05:25,688
The verification worker discovers new tools and checks vendor pages for prices and features.

80
00:05:25,688 --> 00:05:30,215
The renderer is a sandboxed Chromium for pages that need JavaScript.

81
00:05:30,215 --> 00:05:35,874
It can only reach the internet through a guarded proxy that blocks private addresses.

82
00:05:35,874 --> 00:05:42,930
Redis keeps chat history per account, and SQLite holds the catalog, plans, receipts and the spend ledgers.

83
00:05:43,580 --> 00:05:45,186
Sign-in uses Supabase.

84
00:05:45,186 --> 00:05:51,244
We verify the actual token with Supabase on every request, we don't just decode it.

85
00:05:51,244 --> 00:05:54,820
The model is Claude Haiku 4.5 through OpenRouter.

86
00:05:55,470 --> 00:05:58,119
This is the flow for one Arkadio answer.

87
00:05:58,119 --> 00:06:01,696
The app registers what it expects before Arkadio runs.

88
00:06:01,696 --> 00:06:06,729
Arkadio calls the tool, the tool writes a receipt with a hash of the result.

89
00:06:06,729 --> 00:06:11,167
When Arkadio finishes, the app checks that receipt and hash itself.

90
00:06:11,167 --> 00:06:13,750
A model saying success is never enough.

91
00:06:14,400 --> 00:06:15,893
And the budget is real.

92
00:06:15,893 --> 00:06:17,386
We set lifetime limits:

93
00:06:17,386 --> 00:06:22,515
four dollars for chat, one dollar for discovery, ten dollars total for testing.

94
00:06:22,515 --> 00:06:26,670
The whole project so far has used about four and a half dollars.

95
00:06:27,320 --> 00:06:32,420
We want to be honest about what broke, because finding these is what made the product trustworthy.

96
00:06:33,070 --> 00:06:33,305
One.

97
00:06:33,305 --> 00:06:35,834
Tools looked recommended when they weren't.

98
00:06:35,834 --> 00:06:45,889
When we checked one tool for a task, like Homebase for restaurant scheduling, the page showed the tools next to it as Also a good fit, even though nobody had checked them.

99
00:06:45,889 --> 00:06:57,533
We ran an audit of two hundred and forty business and task combinations, and found twenty-five where this happened, including medical staff scheduling, which is exactly where a wrong answer matters.

100
00:06:57,533 --> 00:06:59,297
We fixed it at the data level.

101
00:06:59,297 --> 00:07:05,530
Each tool now carries which tasks it was checked for, and the page only says Recommended when that's true.

102
00:07:06,180 --> 00:07:06,434
Two.

103
00:07:06,434 --> 00:07:08,781
The page was full of not sure labels.

104
00:07:08,781 --> 00:07:12,968
Cards used to say things like Needs checking, or Source not found.

105
00:07:12,968 --> 00:07:16,204
That's honest, but it's noise for a business owner.

106
00:07:16,204 --> 00:07:19,059
Now if we don't know a fact, we leave it off.

107
00:07:19,059 --> 00:07:23,500
The full record, with sources and dates, is still kept in the backend.

108
00:07:24,150 --> 00:07:24,519
Three.

109
00:07:24,519 --> 00:07:26,673
The carousel changed your business.

110
00:07:26,673 --> 00:07:35,410
If you typed Restaurant and then clicked a shortcut on a coffee shop slide, your business quietly became Coffee shop, and the results changed.

111
00:07:35,410 --> 00:07:37,010
Now what you type is kept.

112
00:07:37,660 --> 00:07:37,988
Four.

113
00:07:37,988 --> 00:07:41,531
Arkadio could describe features a tool never returned.

114
00:07:41,531 --> 00:07:47,042
In early tests, the model wrote nice sounding descriptions that weren't in our data.

115
00:07:47,042 --> 00:07:52,226
That's why we added the receipt and evidence check you saw in the architecture.

116
00:07:52,226 --> 00:07:55,900
Arkadio can only say what the catalog actually returned.

117
00:07:56,550 --> 00:07:56,874
Five.

118
00:07:56,874 --> 00:07:58,947
Prices that were really guesses.

119
00:07:58,947 --> 00:08:04,195
Some vendor pages show annual or per-seat prices, or hide them behind JavaScript.

120
00:08:04,195 --> 00:08:11,580
We now only accept a monthly price when the plan, the price and the currency all come from the same official page.

121
00:08:11,580 --> 00:08:14,560
If it's unclear, we don't show a price at all.

122
00:08:15,210 --> 00:08:18,091
And if we had sixteen weeks, here's the plan.

123
00:08:18,091 --> 00:08:19,244
Weeks one to four:

124
00:08:19,244 --> 00:08:19,628
depth.

125
00:08:19,628 --> 00:08:27,056
Grow the checked catalog from a few dozen tools to a few hundred, with automatic re-checks so prices never go stale.

126
00:08:27,056 --> 00:08:28,337
Weeks five to eight:

127
00:08:28,337 --> 00:08:29,105
real owners.

128
00:08:29,105 --> 00:08:34,804
Pilots with local shops, adding what they ask for, like sourced reviews and setup guides.

129
00:08:34,804 --> 00:08:36,149
Weeks nine to twelve:

130
00:08:36,149 --> 00:08:37,301
Arkadio does more.

131
00:08:37,301 --> 00:08:43,128
Connecting the tools an owner already uses, more languages, and an outside security review.

132
00:08:43,128 --> 00:08:45,049
And weeks thirteen to sixteen:

133
00:08:45,049 --> 00:08:45,498
launch.

134
00:08:45,498 --> 00:08:51,581
Ready for many users at once, a sustainable cost per conversation, and one number that matters:

135
00:08:51,581 --> 00:08:54,270
how much time and money we saved an owner.

136
00:08:54,920 --> 00:08:56,098
So that's Creed A.I.

137
00:08:56,098 --> 00:08:58,630
You tell it your business and your problem.

138
00:08:58,630 --> 00:09:08,172
It shows you tools that were actually checked for that job, the real monthly cost and the source, and Arkadio helps you think it through without making things up.

139
00:09:08,172 --> 00:09:12,942
Next, we're adding sourced customer reviews and making long chats more resilient.

140
00:09:12,942 --> 00:09:14,120
Thanks for watching.

